Hello, welcome back to our course and let us continue talking about graphics in R.

00:00:06.802 --> 00:00:10.802
So imagine now that I would like to have a plot which distinguishes

00:00:17.626 --> 00:00:21.626
different factors over a factor variable. So for example here. in that dataset iris, you might remember that we had two different species of irises.

00:00:21.632 --> 00:00:28.632
And now I would like to create a plot where these two different species are separated.

00:00:29.015 --> 00:00:35.015
So here, for example, I could change the style of a point, and I could change,

00:00:43.381 --> 00:00:48.381
a color depending on the particular species of this iris. So for example, how can I do it? Well, I could select a particular type of a point, so here I'm selecting

00:00:56.236 --> 00:00:59.236
type 16, 2 and 3. And I'm selecting a color, and I'm selecting colors 16, 2 and 3. And then, basically, here.

00:01:09.554 --> 00:01:12.554
If you look into Iris$species. You will see that actually this is a factor variable with 3 different levels. So basically, what does it mean? It means that I'm assigning the first

00:01:12.657 --> 00:01:20.657
style to the first level of that factor variable, the second style to

00:01:21.177 --> 00:01:26.177
the second level and the third style to the to the third level. Similarly for

00:01:29.849 --> 00:01:33.849
colors. And now, once I plot it. I would like to have a length on the x-axis.

00:01:33.954 --> 00:01:45.954
width on the y-axis and different colors and different styles, and then let us see how it looks like. It looks as follows, so basically you can see that we have 3 different

00:01:49.073 --> 00:01:53.073
species separated by different colors. And different types of points.

00:01:59.812 --> 00:02:02.812
Of course you could choose whatever color and point you like from the ones which are available in R.

00:02:07.991 --> 00:02:13.991
Alright. So, another thing, interesting thing, is to plot. Different graphics next to each other. For example to arrange them.

00:02:22.882 --> 00:02:25.882
in the way we like. One way to do it is to use this mfrow option in the function parameters. So here you can see that apart from passing

00:02:25.572 --> 00:02:32.572
our usual parameter, bg="grey", so background. I'm passing also mfrow.

00:02:32.849 --> 00:02:37.849
And then I'm saying to have one row, so 1, and then two

00:02:41.558 --> 00:02:43.558
corresponds to two columns, one, two. And then, now, if I plot two

00:02:49.197 --> 00:02:51.197
different plots. So basically, I plot pressure with type h, a solid line.

00:02:57.081 --> 00:02:59.081
And then pressure with type h. With this vertical bars, then you can see that they are arranged in a way

00:02:59.034 --> 00:03:05.034
I wanted them to be arranged, because I select that particular option in mfrow.

00:03:11.558 --> 00:03:17.558
there is also alternative, maybe more flexible alternative, through a function layout. So, in layout, I can basically select

00:03:22.477 --> 00:03:25.477
how I would like to have my plots arranged. Irrespective of,

00:03:29.613 --> 00:03:31.613
of when they appear in my plotting window. So what do I mean by that? So now, here

00:03:32.454 --> 00:03:42.454
look at the layout function. So here we have this layout function, and then I'm passing there a matrix. So matrix with 4 elements

00:03:44.646 --> 00:03:46.646
from 1 to 4. And with two rows,

00:03:47.140 --> 00:03:53.140
1, 2, and then two columns, 1, 2, and then I would like to have these numbers

00:03:58.501 --> 00:04:01.501
arranged byrow. So, I have 1, 2, 3, 4. These numbers

00:04:09.198 --> 00:04:12.198
refer to a particular plot I'm constructing. And then here you can see we are showing a layout with 4 panes, 1, 2, 3, 4.

00:04:12.967 --> 00:04:17.967
But now imagine that I would like to have a completely different layout. I would like to have a layout for

00:04:25.637 --> 00:04:27.637
three plots. And then this could be also created using a layout function, this time in my matrix, I have 3 numbers.

00:04:34.985 --> 00:04:37.985
1, 2, and 3, but. I would like to have the first plot in that first plotting window, the big one.

00:04:38.359 --> 00:04:43.359
Then, I would like to have my third plot in the next pane.

00:04:42.749 --> 00:04:48.749
And then I would like to have my second plot actually in the last pane.

00:04:49.055 --> 00:04:53.055
And then here, I'm showing the layout with 3 different

00:04:53.158 --> 00:04:59.158
panes. So you can see that, indeed, we could, like, basically arrange our plots in any way we want.

00:05:00.214 --> 00:05:10.214
alright, so now let us talk about box plots. So box plots are useful for visualizing the distribution of data, range, variability, outliers.

00:05:09.996 --> 00:05:16.996
So, here, how can I create a box plot? So basically, I could use that function boxplot.

00:05:22.015 --> 00:05:26.015
And then, I would like to create a box plot for lengths of

00:05:25.496 --> 00:05:30.496
samples, so what can I see in this box plot? This, solid

00:05:30.390 --> 00:05:38.390
black line, refers to median. Here we have a bottom and the top of a box. These two refer to

00:05:38.268 --> 00:05:44.268
quartiles, then we also have whiskers here and there. These refer to 1.5

00:05:43.642 --> 00:05:50.642
from my, quartiles, so basically from, from the range.

00:05:50.851 --> 00:05:55.851
And then, if I had outliers, they would be above or below the whiskers.

00:05:57.459 --> 00:06:07.459
we could also have a few box plots in the same plotting window. So here for example, I could also pass to Boxplot

00:06:08.324 --> 00:06:13.324
the data, pressure data, and then if I pass

00:06:12.975 --> 00:06:17.975
a data frame to a box plot, then you can see that I have two box plots.

00:06:27.016 --> 00:06:29.016
they are now in a vertical position, and then I have a temperature, I have a pressure, and indeed, I also can change

00:06:29.068 --> 00:06:38.068
the color of a box plot, like here I selected light gray, and then you can see that, indeed, for pressure, we have a few outliers, because they are above the

00:06:43.975 --> 00:06:45.975
top whisker. We could also have

00:06:45.364 --> 00:06:52.364
box plots separated by factors, so how can I do it? So here, if I now select in my function box plot

00:06:58.688 --> 00:07:01.688
that I would like to have the length in the box plot, but I would like to have this length to be separated

00:07:02.450 --> 00:07:07.450
by different species of irises. So basically, I use this ~ notation.

00:07:16.935 --> 00:07:19.935
So, you might remember that species were these factors in the factor variable and I would like to have lengths separated by species, so meaning I would like to have 3 different box plots.

00:07:25.677 --> 00:07:28.677
And I'm passing here Data Iris. Now I would like to have boxplot

00:07:27.117 --> 00:07:35.117
Sorted out horizontally, and I would like to assign different colors, 3 colors, red, blue, and green.

00:07:34.940 --> 00:07:42.940
And now I'm also inserting a legend to my plotting window, so here you can see that I´m inserting the legend at

00:07:48.721 --> 00:07:50.721
bottom right, bottom right. Instead of selecting a particular location on X and Y axis.

00:07:57.195 --> 00:08:01.195
I'm also here, selecting different styles of points. You can see in the legend.

00:08:00.734 --> 00:08:12.734
it's 2, and then I'm also selecting different colors, red, blue, green. So you can see that the colors correspond to different

00:08:12.514 --> 00:08:20.514
species, but actually, here, for the style of the points here, they are all the same, they're triangles.

00:08:20.715 --> 00:08:28.715
You could have selected anything you wanted, and then in the legend, I also selected the names of these species. So we have 3 different names.

00:08:33.055 --> 00:08:38.055
we could now see how our three plots would have been arranged.

00:08:38.453 --> 00:08:48.453
If our layout had been chosen as follows, yes, so you might remember that that was our first plot, so indeed this is the first plot in the first big pane of our plotting window.

00:08:49.376 --> 00:08:55.376
Then, we wanted to have our third plot, that was our last plot here, and then we wanted to have the second plot

00:08:57.419 --> 00:09:01.419
in the last plotting area of our plotting window.

00:09:02.321 --> 00:09:08.321
Okay, now let us talk about histograms. So histograms are also useful for visualizing

00:09:08.111 --> 00:09:17.111
the distribution of data and here we could see a histogram with different frequencies.

00:09:19.978 --> 00:09:22.978
So basically, here we can see that. On the x-axis, I have the sepal length.

00:09:22.604 --> 00:09:28.604
And then on the y-axis, I have frequency, so meaning how

00:09:33.396 --> 00:09:36.396
many observations where in that particular category? So, in the category for

00:09:39.290 --> 00:09:44.290
Sample length between 4 and 4.5. so I had 5. Then, for the sepal length between

00:09:53.743 --> 00:09:56.743
4.5 and 5 I had around 27, etc, etc. Another way to create a histogram is with density. So, if we assume that these.

00:10:03.180 --> 00:10:07.180
Lengths, where random variables from a normal distribution for example. Then we could also,

00:10:08.895 --> 00:10:16.895
here, instead of frequencies, select a density. So how can I do it? Well, I could just, change this freq parameter

00:10:17.068 --> 00:10:27.068
from, true to false, and then you can see, instead of frequencies, I have a density, which corresponds to a density of my random variables.

00:10:27.202 --> 00:10:33.202
Now, another option is to add, on top of that,

00:10:42.377 --> 00:10:44.377
density created from histograms. I would also like to have a curve of a density. So, here, let us expand a little bit our plot.

00:10:52.140 --> 00:10:55.140
So once again, I have a histogram. For the length of the sepals in the iris data, the frequency is selected to be false

00:10:55.077 --> 00:11:02.077
and now I'm also adjusting the limits of my y-axis from 0 to 0.6.

00:11:02.459 --> 00:11:07.459
Then I'm also adjusting the title of my xlab, so basically my

00:11:07.429 --> 00:11:14.429
X-axis, and then the main title of the histogram is NULL. I don't want to have any title.

00:11:14.062 --> 00:11:21.062
And then, as I said, we could now superimpose on that histogram

00:11:20.711 --> 00:11:26.711
a curve with the density. How can I do it? I could first select this

00:11:30.250 --> 00:11:34.250
grid of points. And I would like them to be passed then

00:11:33.935 --> 00:11:40.935
to the lines function. So here, for example, you can see that I'm defining this.

00:11:46.144 --> 00:11:50.144
Vector G. This vector is defined, Through seq function, so the minimum is the minimum of the length.

00:11:52.621 --> 00:11:56.621
The maximum is also a maximum of the length. And then I would like to have 100 elements.

00:12:03.037 --> 00:12:06.037
And then here, you can see that now i'm constructing

00:12:09.652 --> 00:12:11.652
the density for these points. So basically I'm now applying function

00:12:16.809 --> 00:12:20.809
density of normal distributed random variable for points in vector G and then that density

00:12:25.463 --> 00:12:30.463
corresponds to the random variable with mean defined through the mean of the lengths of sepals.

00:12:29.665 --> 00:12:36.665
And through the standard deviation of the length of sepals.

00:12:42.291 --> 00:12:46.291
And then, here, I could add the density line to a plot, so we can see that now I would superimpose that line

00:12:46.327 --> 00:12:54.327
where on X axis, we have G, on the y-axis, we have the density, and then the color of the slide is red3

00:12:57.642 --> 00:13:01.642
and then, the width of the line is 2. And then you can see, indeed, that now I have a histogram

00:13:01.704 --> 00:13:10.704
with density, and then the density line superimposed on that histogram. Let me stop here, and then we are going to come back.

